{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias in rising regimes: when the recent history shows an upswing (e.g., multiple higher closes / breakout), the model under-forecasts the level over the next ~5–20 days. Apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small in days 1–3, larger in days ~7–20).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the model reacts too slowly to sharp upside moves; it predicts a flatter path while realized prices keep trending up. Add a momentum term based on the last 5–10 day slope to lift the mid-horizon trajectory.",
    "Underestimation of peak magnitude after upside shocks: around spike events, the model captures direction but not amplitude (realized peaks exceed forecast). Inflate forecast variance/peaks when recent daily range/volatility is elevated.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot after regime change: when the true market turns down after a run-up, the model can keep extrapolating the prior rise and ends up too high in the last ~5–10 forecast days. Introduce mean-reversion damping beyond ~day 20, especially if history shows stretched levels vs recent average.",
    "Horizon asymmetry: near-term (days 1–5) errors are smaller and often slightly low; mid-horizon (days 6–20) shows the largest negative bias in uptrends; far-horizon (days 21–30) can flip to positive bias if a reversal occurs. Use regime detection (trend vs reversal) to switch from momentum-boost (mid) to mean-reversion (far)."
  ]
}
